WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.900
Now, I'd like to introduce our featured presenters today,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.155
Dr. Louis Halamek and Dr. Nicole Yamada.

00:00:16.155 --> 00:00:18.840
Dr. Halamek is a Professor in the Division of

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.050
Neonatal and Developmental Medicine in the Department of Pediatrics at

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:24.780
Stanford Medicine and he's a Director of

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.245
Neonatal Resuscitation at Packard Children's Hospital.

00:00:28.245 --> 00:00:32.880
Dr. Halamek is the Founding Director of the Center for Advanced Pediatric and Perinatal

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:37.185
Education and the world's first center dedicated to fetal,

00:00:37.185 --> 00:00:41.400
neonatal, pediatric and obstetrics simulation-based training and research.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.940
He is a former member of the Board of Directors of

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:47.109
the International Pediatric Simulation Society,

00:00:47.109 --> 00:00:51.180
the Inaugural Board of Directors of the Society for Simulation in Health Care,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.805
and the Inaugural Editorial Board of Simulation in Health Care.

00:00:54.805 --> 00:00:59.455
His career in simulation-based training and research began in 1995,

00:00:59.455 --> 00:01:00.950
and over the past two decades,

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.050
he's refined a method of debriefing based on

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:05.495
his collaboration with colleagues in aerospace,

00:01:05.495 --> 00:01:07.810
aviation, and the military.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:12.080
This objective professional approach to debriefing has been adopted as

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.765
a standard for the neonatal resuscitation program of the American Academy of Pediatrics,

00:01:16.765 --> 00:01:20.700
it's 25,000 instructors, and 3.3 million trainees.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.930
Dr Yamada is a Clinical Assistant Professor in the Division of

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:28.375
Neonatal and Developmental Medicine in the Department of Pediatrics at Stanford Medicine,

00:01:28.375 --> 00:01:30.920
and is an attending neonatologist in

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.010
the Intensive Care Unit at Packard Children's Hospital.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.620
Dr. Yamada is the associate director of

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:39.800
the Center for Advanced pediatric and perinatal education.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.175
She is an expert in debriefing teams of healthcare professionals from

00:01:43.175 --> 00:01:46.655
all areas of clinical care and of all levels of experience,

00:01:46.655 --> 00:01:47.960
and she's worked with teams from

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.000
both academic and private hospitals

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.885
all over the US as well as teams from around the world.

00:01:52.885 --> 00:01:54.350
And she's been invited to deliver

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.440
simulation instructor programs in Moscow and Hong Kong.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.495
And with that, let's turn it over to Dr. Halamek and Dr. Yamada.

00:02:01.495 --> 00:02:05.135
Thank you Jackie for those kind introductions.

00:02:05.135 --> 00:02:07.385
I'd like to emphasize the fact that both

00:02:07.385 --> 00:02:09.890
Dr. Yamada and I are practicing neonatologists,

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:12.995
so we look at the debriefing from the vantage point of

00:02:12.995 --> 00:02:14.795
healthcare professionals who are leading

00:02:14.795 --> 00:02:18.570
teams of people in the care of critically ill patients.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.260
Um, I do want to acknowledge that

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.860
our training and debriefing strategies as you mentioned have been

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:28.825
heavily influenced by colleagues in other industries who are outside of healthcare.

00:02:28.825 --> 00:02:34.440
And were- in those industries the expectations for both human performance are high,

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.725
as well as the risk to human life is high.

00:02:36.725 --> 00:02:41.510
And especially like to say thanks to people that we've known over the years,

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:45.520
both at NASA and the FAA for whom- from whom we've learned so much.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.650
Um, I'd also like to point out that while our discussion today is

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.605
really focused on health care debriefing, um,

00:02:51.605 --> 00:02:54.140
the strategies that we're going to highlight over the course of

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:58.175
the next 40 minutes or so can be applied when debriefing any type of situation.

00:02:58.175 --> 00:03:01.190
And that's going to be especially important for our audience, uh,

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.710
today because we directly saw that

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:07.740
approximately half of you are actually working in industries outside of healthcare.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.365
Okay. So let's get started the learning objectives as many of you are

00:03:14.365 --> 00:03:18.280
aware from when you signed up for the webinar are these listed here.

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.345
Um, and they're really specifically related to our selection

00:03:22.345 --> 00:03:27.250
of wanting to discuss the really foundational strategies that we think are,

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:31.420
uh, instrumental to funding and effective debriefing.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.180
The outline for our webinars.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.520
First, we'll just start with what is debriefing,

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.550
a brief definition there so that we're all on the same page about,

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:41.650
um, what we're talking about today.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.710
And then we'll first cover some guiding principles about how

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:48.655
we generally think about debriefings and our approach to debriefings,

00:03:48.655 --> 00:03:50.395
um, in any situation.

00:03:50.395 --> 00:03:53.180
And then finally we'll roll into a discussion of

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:57.200
four specific key strategies with video examples of,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.230
um, things that we specifically implement through,

00:04:00.230 --> 00:04:01.820
um, all of our debriefings.

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:04.160
I think it's worth mentioning that these are

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.890
only just four strategies out of a total of 20,

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.570
and we cover all 20 in our online debriefing program,

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:12.380
which you'll hear about later at the end of this webinar.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.910
But these aren't all of the- the solutions to running an effective debriefing,

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:18.110
we just wanted to cover what we think is really

00:04:18.110 --> 00:04:22.110
instrumental and foundational during this discussion.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:27.455
So the first question I think is worth answering is what is a debriefing?

00:04:27.455 --> 00:04:31.130
And so for those of you who may not be familiar with what debriefings are,

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.900
we really wanted to make sure that we're all on

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.570
the same page about what we're discussing here today.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:38.480
So debriefing at a very basic level is

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:41.915
an interactive discussion of a prior series of events

00:04:41.915 --> 00:04:44.930
and can be facilitated in a number of different ways

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.560
into different levels of involvement from the debriefer.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.505
And oftentimes that depends on the experience level of those people being debriefed.

00:04:52.505 --> 00:04:55.370
So people who may have a lot of experience, um,

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.530
with debriefings may be able to even start to

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.600
conduct their own self debriefings as a- as a team,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.715
whereas other people who may be less familiar with the,

00:05:03.715 --> 00:05:05.355
uh, role and the,

00:05:05.355 --> 00:05:08.690
um function of a debriefing may need a little bit more facilitation

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:13.040
from a trained debriefer or someone with experience with debriefing.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:17.010
The next question is what is a good debriefing?

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.550
So now we know what a debriefing is,

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:21.760
but how do you make it- how do you make a good debriefing?

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:25.120
A good debriefing is one that is constructive,

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:29.155
concise, and identifies both strengths and weaknesses.

00:05:29.155 --> 00:05:31.675
And oftentimes especially those of us coming from

00:05:31.675 --> 00:05:35.560
an environment where we're treating other people or we're educators,

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.500
we often focus on what people need to learn,

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.300
what they might need to improve on.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.060
And one of the things that we really want to emphasize is that

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:45.820
the- a good debriefing is an opportunity to talk about

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:48.940
both strengths and weaknesses and helping those

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:52.270
that are being debriefed understand what facilitated

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.700
their ability to perform really well if they had

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:59.210
a really strong performance and how to replicate that performance again in the future,

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:03.290
mm, and also then taking opportunity to address weaknesses.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:04.790
Uh, and not only identifying them,

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:09.230
but coming up with strategies to help those people who are being debriefed, um,

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:14.125
learn how to avoid those same challenges or the same weaknesses again in the future.

00:06:14.125 --> 00:06:21.200
So I'll let Lou talk about some of the guiding principles that we have for debriefing.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.305
So we'll go ahead and review those now.

00:06:24.305 --> 00:06:27.680
The first guiding principle is that instructors

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:30.830
should always maintain a professional business-like,

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:35.855
matter-of-fact tone whether trainee performance was exemplary or highly flawed.

00:06:35.855 --> 00:06:39.424
Uh, our overall goal is to enable

00:06:39.424 --> 00:06:43.490
the people being debriefed to eventually be able to be brief themselves.

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:46.520
Uh, we want them to be able to do this

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:49.850
without the need for a trained debriefer, certainly in health care.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:52.220
It's not always going to be possible if we're debriefing

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:55.475
real-life situations to have a trained debriefer present,

00:06:55.475 --> 00:06:58.910
and so we want to allow them opportunity to practice this and we want

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.690
to draw as much information from them and self critique as we can,

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.670
uh, during that debriefing.

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:08.600
Uh, that ability to assess their own performance becomes extremely important,

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:11.620
especially when we were working in the real environment.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.230
Second guiding principle, the role of the instructor in a briefing is to

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:18.920
facilitate rather than dominate discussion among trainees.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.010
Uh, when I lead a debriefing,

00:07:21.010 --> 00:07:23.660
my expectation that those being debriefed will

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:26.480
actually do most of the talking and I can be relatively quiet.

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.240
Now, that doesn't mean that if a team does not recognize

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:33.029
a performance gap that I'm not going to eventually intervene,

00:07:33.029 --> 00:07:35.060
I only intervene only after I've

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:39.260
tried as much as I possibly can to draw that information,

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.970
draw that self critique out from them.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:48.175
Debriefing should be focused on the actions of the individual trainees.

00:07:48.175 --> 00:07:51.310
How those actions contributed to the performance of the team.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:54.415
How team performance influenced patient outcome.

00:07:54.415 --> 00:07:57.025
Uh, that is absolutely critical,

00:07:57.025 --> 00:08:00.205
we have to get to what happened to the patient,

00:08:00.205 --> 00:08:03.115
you know how the actions of the team and actually lead to that.

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:06.520
Uh, in the end what we want is we want the people being debriefed

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.570
to develop strategies for replicating those actions that,

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:13.540
uh, result in good care of the patient.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:15.925
In other words, successful human and system performance,

00:08:15.925 --> 00:08:20.080
but also to avoid actions that are ineffective or harmful.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.110
So we again, we really want them to,uh,

00:08:23.110 --> 00:08:27.040
be able to reflect and develop these strategies themselves.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:30.625
And there should always be in any health care debriefing,

00:08:30.625 --> 00:08:34.010
uh, a discussion of what happened to the patient.

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:37.345
This is a real key point, uh,

00:08:37.345 --> 00:08:41.440
technical performance to briefings which is what Dr. Yamada and I did.

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:45.940
Uh, these are different than critical incident stress debriefings.

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:49.225
These two types of debriefings have different objectives,

00:08:49.225 --> 00:08:52.930
and no attempts should ever be made to try to conduct them simultaneously.

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:56.155
And a technical performance debriefing is not therapy.

00:08:56.155 --> 00:08:58.240
Technical performance debriefings and

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:00.190
critical incident stress debrie- debriefings are

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.965
often confused in health care that's been our experience.

00:09:02.965 --> 00:09:07.570
And the way to distinguish these two is at a technical performance debriefing

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:12.520
is focused ultimately on what affected the care of the patient and how the patient did.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.520
Whereas a critical incident stress debriefing is focused on

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:18.760
the emotional health of those who are caring for the patient.

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:20.845
Now, both of those are very important,

00:09:20.845 --> 00:09:22.510
both of them have their place,

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:27.879
but it can mean they have very distinct and very different objectives,

00:09:27.879 --> 00:09:31.880
and so don't confuse those two when you are debriefing.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.580
Okay. So we'll roll into now our discussion of

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:43.255
these four key strategies that we really wanted to dig into today.

00:09:43.255 --> 00:09:46.465
Um, and again, these are just four of 20.

00:09:46.465 --> 00:09:48.820
But what we'll- what we'll do for the, um,

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:53.260
next portion of the webinar is actually introduce each strategy followed by

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:58.990
a video clip from a debriefing showing that strategy being implemented by a debriefer.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:02.890
And then we'll have a little discussion afterwards about really why we feel

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:04.600
that those strategies are important and what we're

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:07.325
hoping to achieve with- with the each- each of those.

00:10:07.325 --> 00:10:10.510
Um, just to comment on the clips that you'll see,

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:15.310
these were taken from debriefings that were recorded to illustrate specific points.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:17.080
Um, so while the actors, uh,

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:19.810
in these debriefings were asked to portray

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:21.790
specific roles for both the scenarios in

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:24.310
which they performed and then the subsequent debriefing.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.475
They're all practicing healthcare professionals, um,

00:10:27.475 --> 00:10:31.240
but the debriefer himself was not scripted and really

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:35.930
implemented these strategies as they would be in any debriefing.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:38.800
The first strategy, again,

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.280
is one that I think applies to all debriefings and it's really just how to

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:47.005
prepare learners or people being debriefed to participate in that debriefing.

00:10:47.005 --> 00:10:50.320
Um, and we want to show how you can clearly

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.220
communicate expectations at the very beginning of a debriefing.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.605
So in this clip [NOISE] it's the first 40 seconds

00:10:56.605 --> 00:10:59.260
of the first debriefing with this group of,

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:04.390
uh, people being debriefed and the debriefer who's standing to lead that discussion.

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:07.570
Anyone else's performance outside of the space.

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:10.480
The conversation certainly is open to everyone.

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:14.440
So even if you weren't playing a role in that particular phase of the- the scenario,

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:16.569
please feel free to chime in with any observations,

00:11:16.569 --> 00:11:20.440
uh, or thoughts you may have, and be open to learning.

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.605
Especially be open to learning from my mistakes, um,

00:11:23.605 --> 00:11:28.105
be able to accept any critique both gratefully and gracefully.

00:11:28.105 --> 00:11:32.665
And then finally, we have a lot of potential things that we could discuss,

00:11:32.665 --> 00:11:35.005
a lot things that we could potentially discuss,

00:11:35.005 --> 00:11:37.180
uh, but won't have time to do- go through all those today.

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:39.650
So we'll try to hit the major points.

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:43.090
Okay. So Dr. Halamek,

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:45.880
what are your goals as the debriefer when you

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:49.525
make these types of statements at the beginning of a debriefing?

00:11:49.525 --> 00:11:53.470
Well, I think as you could see and hear in that particular video segment,

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:55.270
uh, I have some very specific goals,

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:56.845
things I wanna communicate, uh,

00:11:56.845 --> 00:11:58.855
at the beginning of every debriefing,

00:11:58.855 --> 00:12:01.120
um, when I'm first meeting this group.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:04.840
Um, first of all I want to ensure that absolute confidentiality is going

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:08.649
to be maintained, um, during, uh,

00:12:08.649 --> 00:12:12.325
any sort of scenario whether it's a simulated scenario or a real scenario,uh,

00:12:12.325 --> 00:12:16.990
you may see weaknesses become manifest and certainly those are things

00:12:16.990 --> 00:12:21.595
that we want to keep confidential within the group, that's being debriefed.

00:12:21.595 --> 00:12:24.025
We want to ensure that there's psychological safety,

00:12:24.025 --> 00:12:28.210
meaning that actually people feel safe and comfortable talking about their mistakes,

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:31.535
uh, within the group that's, uh, assembled.

00:12:31.535 --> 00:12:33.280
Uh, I would like to see

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:36.640
active participation from all members of the group although I don't mandate it.

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.010
I know that some people actually are quiet, uh,

00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:43.420
by nature and as long as it appears that they are mentally engaged and are learning,

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:46.580
I don't push people to talk but I certainly would like to see that.

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:50.560
Um, I really want people to be open to learning from mistakes.

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:53.170
If we are running simulated scenarios,

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:56.890
uh, in general those are about making mistakes and learning from those.

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:00.565
So that's a real key expectation that we set up cramped.

00:13:00.565 --> 00:13:06.130
Um, and what I talked about at the very end that everyone will accept critique,

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:08.155
and remember critique is not criticism,

00:13:08.155 --> 00:13:11.590
but that everyone will accept critique both gratefully and gracefully.

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:13.300
Yeah. And I have to thank, uh,

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.835
that my colleague and friend at Johnson Space Center,

00:13:15.835 --> 00:13:19.060
Mike Sterling for that because that is sort of the mantra that they

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:22.795
live by when they debrief the astronaut crews and flight controllers.

00:13:22.795 --> 00:13:26.860
And then finally, especially if you are working in the real environment, uh,

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:31.840
where you- you don't have a lot of time to debrief after a real event has occurred

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:34.210
with patient care because the teams

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:36.610
have to go back and take care of other patients in that unit.

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:41.770
Um, so for us we were debriefing issues within the ICU or

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:44.560
within labor and delivery then we definitely need to make sure that

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:47.500
people understand we only have a few minutes for this debriefing,

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:50.650
therefore, we're going to cover the most of- what we think are the most important topics,

00:13:50.650 --> 00:13:52.090
and then we're going to move on.

00:13:52.090 --> 00:13:57.115
Okay. And that's something that you cover to beginning of every debriefing?

00:13:57.115 --> 00:13:58.975
Well, it- it depends, uh,

00:13:58.975 --> 00:14:00.790
if it's a first time we're meeting a group,

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:02.350
and certainly I'll talk about those things.

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:05.275
I want to make sure that my expectations are clear to them, uh,

00:14:05.275 --> 00:14:07.690
and they know that this is going to be an environment in which they

00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:10.300
can freely talk about the mistakes and learn from them.

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:12.760
Uh- on the other hand if we're running a training program or

00:14:12.760 --> 00:14:15.160
maybe we're running four or five scenarios, uh,

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.695
over the course of the morning or the day, uh,

00:14:17.695 --> 00:14:20.170
then I won't repeat those every time,

00:14:20.170 --> 00:14:24.115
as long as I'm sure that people under- understood them, heard them the first time.

00:14:24.115 --> 00:14:27.595
Okay. [NOISE]. All right.

00:14:27.595 --> 00:14:31.450
And so then moving into actually initiating the debriefing itself.

00:14:31.450 --> 00:14:34.510
Um, one of the things that we always talk about,

00:14:34.510 --> 00:14:36.520
and when we talk about how to really

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:38.860
run an effective debriefing is starting with this question.

00:14:38.860 --> 00:14:40.825
What happened in 10 words or less?

00:14:40.825 --> 00:14:44.905
And so this next clip will show this first question being asked by the debriefer.

00:14:44.905 --> 00:14:49.280
And then the initial responses given by the people being debriefed.

00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:56.275
To start off, uh, what happened in that scenario in 10 words or less?

00:14:56.275 --> 00:15:01.810
Term neonate, umbilical cord rupture, needed blood.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.015
Okay.

00:15:03.015 --> 00:15:06.385
I would say term neonate,

00:15:06.385 --> 00:15:10.675
um, hypoxic, hypovolemic, for resuscitation.

00:15:10.675 --> 00:15:13.030
Okay. Anyone else?

00:15:13.030 --> 00:15:15.580
Any other additional input?

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:18.040
I think that included everything.

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:20.069
Okay.

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:24.460
Okay.

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:29.590
Many debriefers in health care had been taught to or

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:32.950
even prefer to start their debriefings with a question along the lines of,

00:15:32.950 --> 00:15:35.095
how did that go, how did that feel?

00:15:35.095 --> 00:15:37.195
Um, and the question here asked was,

00:15:37.195 --> 00:15:39.115
what happened to in 10 minutes or less,

00:15:39.115 --> 00:15:41.170
why do you start debriefing that one? [NOISE]

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:46.180
Um, well, the- that particular question, um, you know,

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:47.755
how did that go, uh,

00:15:47.755 --> 00:15:49.705
for those of us who use video,

00:15:49.705 --> 00:15:52.285
um, it- it's oftentimes it's hard to,

00:15:52.285 --> 00:15:53.680
to know exactly how that went.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:53.890
Uh-huh.

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:55.630
What happened before you've watched the video and have

00:15:55.630 --> 00:15:57.550
a chance to actually assess performance.

00:15:57.550 --> 00:15:59.890
So we try to avoid, uh,

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:01.240
if we're going to be using video,

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:04.180
whether it's a real-life scenario that we're playing video back, uh,

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:06.055
for or it's a simulated scenario,

00:16:06.055 --> 00:16:08.260
we try to avoid getting into the debriefing too

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:11.575
much with an assessment just based solely on memory.

00:16:11.575 --> 00:16:14.935
We got to watch the video and critique it in real time.

00:16:14.935 --> 00:16:16.885
So we avoid that question.

00:16:16.885 --> 00:16:20.740
We also avoid the question is in terms of how did you feel about that.

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:23.500
Um, I know that's very commonly used and people are free to

00:16:23.500 --> 00:16:25.900
choose however they would like to start their debriefing,

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:28.540
but, um, I've not heard that question

00:16:28.540 --> 00:16:32.110
asked [NOISE] in other high-risk industries that do debriefing.

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:36.655
Um, I think that it can set the debriefing up to be confused with the,

00:16:36.655 --> 00:16:38.560
uh, critical incident stress debriefing,

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:40.060
which again as we've talked about earlier,

00:16:40.060 --> 00:16:41.680
a critical incident stress debriefings and

00:16:41.680 --> 00:16:44.545
technical performance debriefings are two very different things,

00:16:44.545 --> 00:16:48.760
and what we're really talking about here today are technical performance debriefings.

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:51.909
And so I try to avoid those kinds of questions

00:16:51.909 --> 00:16:55.765
because they can be rather subjective and at times rather vague.

00:16:55.765 --> 00:16:58.450
So by saying what happened in 10 words or less,

00:16:58.450 --> 00:17:00.850
we try to accomplish a couple of very important things.

00:17:00.850 --> 00:17:02.440
First of all, um,

00:17:02.440 --> 00:17:06.400
we- we try really hard to set a professional business,

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:08.725
as a matter of fact, tone for the debriefing.

00:17:08.725 --> 00:17:11.680
Um, we want people to know that, you know, again,

00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:13.015
saving lives is our business,

00:17:13.015 --> 00:17:14.680
that tone is extremely important,

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:16.585
uh, for the debriefing.

00:17:16.585 --> 00:17:19.225
And we don't want to waste anybody's time, uh,

00:17:19.225 --> 00:17:21.490
with superfluous conversation, especially if we have

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:24.940
very limited time to cover important topics in patient care.

00:17:24.940 --> 00:17:26.740
And so that's the first thing that it does.

00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:29.080
The second thing that it does is it does encourage people to

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:31.630
be concise and precise in our use of language,

00:17:31.630 --> 00:17:35.470
uh, and as we discuss the, uh,

00:17:35.470 --> 00:17:37.015
I've mentioned several times now,

00:17:37.015 --> 00:17:41.530
that especially when debriefing in the real environment where time pressure's quite

00:17:41.530 --> 00:17:46.720
intense and we have after we taking care of one patient and we debrief that,

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:49.000
we have other patients that are waiting for our care.

00:17:49.000 --> 00:17:49.345
Right.

00:17:49.345 --> 00:17:51.250
So we don't have the luxury of,

00:17:51.250 --> 00:17:55.090
of lots and lots of time for- the degree for real situation.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:57.265
Now, we may have that luxury if we're debriefing

00:17:57.265 --> 00:18:00.895
simulated situations in a training program,

00:18:00.895 --> 00:18:03.640
but even there, we don't want to waste time.

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:07.525
Uh, on my experience over the last two-plus decades in using

00:18:07.525 --> 00:18:10.360
simulation healthcare is that what teams really

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:14.290
want is they want to get in these many scenarios as they possibly can.

00:18:14.290 --> 00:18:17.275
So if we are being redundant in our new briefing,

00:18:17.275 --> 00:18:19.270
if at least they're perceiving that perhaps

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:21.250
we're not covering any new material during the debriefing,

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:23.605
we're discussing things aren't terribly important, uh,

00:18:23.605 --> 00:18:28.525
then they start to get antsy and they would much prefer to, uh,

00:18:28.525 --> 00:18:31.450
be in another scenario and see what more they can learn rather

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:35.320
than continuing to talk about things that perhaps aren't as relevant as it should be.

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:37.240
Okay. Uh, but what happened,

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:40.315
in 10 words or less, is a very specific question too.

00:18:40.315 --> 00:18:44.740
Um, sometimes, uh, another strategy that I've seen debrief previews is asking

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:47.440
the questions what went well and what didn't go

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:50.170
well in that scenario and starting out a debriefing that way.

00:18:50.170 --> 00:18:54.550
Can you comment on how that strategy compares to using what happened in 10 words or less?

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:56.973
Again, a lot of these things are personal preference.

00:18:56.973 --> 00:18:57.460
Uh-huh.

00:18:57.460 --> 00:18:59.470
Um, [NOISE] uh, sometimes in the real environment

00:18:59.470 --> 00:19:02.485
when- when time pressure is intense, uh,

00:19:02.485 --> 00:19:05.650
I'll see people say, well, what went well,

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:09.580
they typically start with what went well then they get to what- what didn't go so well.

00:19:09.580 --> 00:19:14.095
Um, the only issue about that is that, um, uh,

00:19:14.095 --> 00:19:16.630
for example, let's say we determine that you and I

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:19.294
are working on a patient and something did not go well.

00:19:19.294 --> 00:19:19.720
Uh-huh.

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:23.440
We say, "Okay, uh, the intubation of that patient did not go well."

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:27.010
Uh, if we don't then drill down,

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:31.735
if we don't get to the underlying causes of why that intubation [NOISE] did not go well,

00:19:31.735 --> 00:19:34.930
you know, it can be something like a deficit in content knowledge.

00:19:34.930 --> 00:19:37.570
Uh, we didn't know what size of endotracheal tube used.

00:19:37.570 --> 00:19:39.955
We didn't know how to properly handle the endoscope.

00:19:39.955 --> 00:19:42.580
It could be a problem with decision-making.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:46.420
Uh, we didn't, um, make the right decision.

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:49.810
We decided to intubate too soon or we decided to intubate too late.

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:53.829
It could be that are- the actual technical skill, hand-eye coordination,

00:19:53.829 --> 00:19:55.630
the way they- we handle the endoscope and

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:59.500
the endotracheal tube wasn't appropriate and didn't allow us to be successful,

00:19:59.500 --> 00:20:03.040
or could involve a host of behavioral skills around communication.

00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:07.075
Maybe we didn't effectively ask for the right size of equipment.

00:20:07.075 --> 00:20:09.220
Uh, maybe the ambient noise of the room is

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:11.080
too high and we weren't able to hear one another.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:14.335
All of those kinds of things and all of those skill sets can actually

00:20:14.335 --> 00:20:18.280
influence why something did not go well or why it went very well,

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:18.640
Right.

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:20.170
And those into being

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:22.240
the critically important aspects of what

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:24.820
happened that you want to cover during any briefing.

00:20:24.820 --> 00:20:28.090
So if you just stay at a very superficial level of, you know,

00:20:28.090 --> 00:20:30.910
tell me three things that went well and three things that didn't go well,

00:20:30.910 --> 00:20:32.770
again, if you have very limited time,

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:34.630
you have only a few minutes to carry a debriefing,

00:20:34.630 --> 00:20:36.265
that gets people thinking about

00:20:36.265 --> 00:20:40.810
those particular aspects but it doesn't drill down to the underlying root causes.

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:43.330
And that's, uh, something that you really need to

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:46.120
be able to get you at some point, uh, during the debriefing.

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:48.010
Okay. So your hope with the- what happened,

00:20:48.010 --> 00:20:50.080
in 10 minutes or less, is just the beginning,

00:20:50.080 --> 00:20:52.390
and then really the rest of the debriefing is to discuss

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:54.925
a lot of those other issues more in specific terms?

00:20:54.925 --> 00:20:56.740
Tha- that's correct, yes.

00:20:56.740 --> 00:21:00.820
Okay. So moving on to the next strategy.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:05.725
This is titled Avoiding Qualitative Statements and it really describes, um,

00:21:05.725 --> 00:21:07.390
our goal of trying to draw

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:11.185
performance assessment from the learners or from the people being debriefed.

00:21:11.185 --> 00:21:14.950
And then just to give the audience some background on the clip that's being used here,

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:18.295
this is taken from a debriefing of the care of a premature infant,

00:21:18.295 --> 00:21:21.190
who is in respiratory failure and in need of CPR.

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:23.905
I mean, at this point, the group is discussing the team's, uh,

00:21:23.905 --> 00:21:26.560
decision of whether to intubate the patient

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:29.845
prior to the initiation of start of chest compressions.

00:21:29.845 --> 00:21:32.590
And we will see that the trainees or the people being

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:35.170
debriefed address a question directly to the debriefer,

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:37.930
and what I want to point your attention to is how the debriefer

00:21:37.930 --> 00:21:42.020
responds and the discussion that follows from the trainees.

00:21:42.870 --> 00:21:49.780
What do you think? Is it difficult to intubate while someone's doing compression?

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:50.935
Have you ever done that?

00:21:50.935 --> 00:21:54.955
Let's- let's see if we have any consensus among the group.

00:21:54.955 --> 00:22:01.120
So the choices are to not do compressions in intubating,

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:02.650
focus on the intubation.

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:05.470
Because it was mentioned that sometimes it can be difficult to

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:09.010
intubate while the chest is moving especially in a very small patient.

00:22:09.010 --> 00:22:14.035
Um, another choice is to start compressions and hold off on intubation,

00:22:14.035 --> 00:22:15.670
perhaps do bag mask.

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:20.480
The other choice is to do both simultaneously.

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:24.040
Do we have any consensus among

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:27.805
this team about what should be done right now with a heart rate of 26,

00:22:27.805 --> 00:22:31.045
four minutes, roughly a little over four minutes in- of age?

00:22:31.045 --> 00:22:33.640
I think if the intubator is comfortable,

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:37.077
it should start with chest compressions and intubation at the same time.

00:22:37.077 --> 00:22:37.690
Okay.

00:22:37.690 --> 00:22:39.355
But if it's starting to interfere,

00:22:39.355 --> 00:22:41.380
then you should hold off on the chest compressions

00:22:41.380 --> 00:22:45.895
because the intubation is most important and only takes a brief moment,

00:22:45.895 --> 00:22:46.210
Good.

00:22:46.210 --> 00:22:49.270
And it does take a while for you to build up,

00:22:49.270 --> 00:22:53.920
um, enough blood flow to have appropriate cerebral perfusion pressure.

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:57.280
Good. Okay. So backup team comes into

00:22:57.280 --> 00:23:04.255
the room and the backup physician is immediately given an order to intubate.

00:23:04.255 --> 00:23:07.730
Please watch what happens from this point. And.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:11.470
Okay. Uh, so in this clip,

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:16.300
we demonstrated this concept of avoiding qualitative statements. What does that mean?

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:21.985
[NOISE] Concretely, it means to avoid use of adjectives and adverbs.

00:23:21.985 --> 00:23:24.190
And the way if you are leading the debriefing

00:23:24.190 --> 00:23:26.110
and the way you're speaking with the team in,

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:31.660
in avoiding- providing any sort of qualitative assessment of their performance.

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:33.820
You know, we've talked about several times already

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:36.190
in the course of our discussion here today that

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:40.975
it's really important for members of these teams to be able to look at their performance,

00:23:40.975 --> 00:23:42.489
reflect on their performance,

00:23:42.489 --> 00:23:43.810
and come up with their own assessment.

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:46.510
[NOISE] Uh, it's not going to be possible to have

00:23:46.510 --> 00:23:49.660
a trained degree for available to run through the hospital

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:53.020
24 hours a day to be present for every debriefing that should be taking

00:23:53.020 --> 00:23:56.620
place [NOISE] and so we need our teams to be able to do this.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:23:59.440
Um, um, I think it's a professionalism issue.

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:01.990
I think it's, uh, issue of- quite honestly,

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:04.000
delivering optimal patient care.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:07.405
So they have to be able to reflect on their own performance,

00:24:07.405 --> 00:24:10.360
um, and decide whether their performance was optimal or not.

00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:12.550
If it wasn't optimal, okay, what are the weaknesses?

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:15.880
Let's identify those weaknesses and let's begin to discuss strategies

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:19.975
for making sure those weaknesses don't become manifest in the future.

00:24:19.975 --> 00:24:24.850
Um, similarly, if it was- if the team performed at a very high level, yeah,

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:30.025
if their performance was deemed to be optimal under the circumstances, uh,

00:24:30.025 --> 00:24:33.340
they really need to understand what it was,

00:24:33.340 --> 00:24:35.980
what it was that enabled them to perform at such a high level.

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:40.135
Because it's- it's one thing to do a really good job at something,

00:24:40.135 --> 00:24:43.918
it's another thing to understand how to replicate that good job in the future.

00:24:43.918 --> 00:24:44.410
Uh-huh.

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:46.090
And so it's not just about weaknesses,

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:47.830
it's not just about good performance,

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:50.995
it's actually about understanding the underlying, uh, uh,

00:24:50.995 --> 00:24:55.075
causes or the, um, uh,

00:24:55.075 --> 00:24:59.350
abilities that, uh, the circumstances that actually allow teams to perform,

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.720
uh, along that, uh, performance spectrum.

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:05.110
Uh-huh. [NOISE] But in this situation, um,

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:09.640
one of the people that being debriefed actually asked you a question directly, um,

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:11.725
and you're an experienced neonatologists

00:25:11.725 --> 00:25:14.680
with expertise in neonatal resuscitation and you probably have

00:25:14.680 --> 00:25:16.780
an opinion in this case whether or not the team

00:25:16.780 --> 00:25:20.065
did the correct thing or the best thing for this patient,

00:25:20.065 --> 00:25:22.120
um, but instead, you've responded with let's see

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:24.430
if we have many consensus from the group.

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:27.050
Why did you avoid answering that question?

00:25:27.050 --> 00:25:29.160
Well, I- I could have easily answered that question.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:29.640
Uh-huh.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:32.730
Um, and the- that team was turning to me as,

00:25:32.730 --> 00:25:35.730
uh, the person that answered the question and tell them how they did.

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:36.480
Uh-huh.

00:25:36.480 --> 00:25:38.790
Um, I don't see that as the role of the debriefer.

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:41.075
Again, unless there is a problem

00:25:41.075 --> 00:25:44.655
that- that they are unable to recognize with their performance,

00:25:44.655 --> 00:25:48.690
where you might need to step in and help them recognize that.

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:49.350
Uh-huh.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:52.830
Um, uh, you really want to draw that answer out from them.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:54.850
You want them to, uh,

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:58.395
assess their own performance as best they possibly can. Um.

00:25:58.395 --> 00:26:04.100
We typically debrief experience health care professionals that the team that- well,

00:26:04.100 --> 00:26:06.020
we seen in that video was, uh, obviously,

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:08.210
a team of experienced health care professionals.

00:26:08.210 --> 00:26:09.650
More than likely there's

00:26:09.650 --> 00:26:13.730
at least one if not more people in the room who know the answer to that question already.

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:15.965
We say they have their own answer formulated.

00:26:15.965 --> 00:26:21.845
And what can happen is if I tell them immediately what I think should have been done,

00:26:21.845 --> 00:26:25.805
I potentially can shut down discussion because they're gonna say, "Oh,

00:26:25.805 --> 00:26:29.210
the debriefer or Doctor Halamek just said this is what should be done.

00:26:29.210 --> 00:26:31.010
I was thinking maybe something different should be done,

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:32.560
but if that's what he says,

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:33.576
then that's what we should go with."

00:26:33.576 --> 00:26:34.015
Uh-huh.

00:26:34.015 --> 00:26:36.970
And I don't wanna make the supposition that since I

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:39.670
wasn't in the scenario that I know exactly what it is they were seeing,

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:40.960
what it is they were hearing,

00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:44.330
and go ahead and tell them what should have been done.

00:26:44.330 --> 00:26:44.525
Uh-huh.

00:26:44.525 --> 00:26:47.360
Because they may have been an alternative strategy that's just as

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:50.750
effective or perhaps even more effective than what I might be talking about.

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:53.990
And so I never wanna shut down that kind of discussion,

00:26:53.990 --> 00:26:55.715
I wanna give it a time to,

00:26:55.715 --> 00:26:57.890
to come up to the surface, uh,

00:26:57.890 --> 00:27:01.760
because oftentimes, the people that are being debriefed have they come up with

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:04.250
some very unique and very valuable solutions to these [NOISE] kinds of

00:27:04.250 --> 00:27:06.920
problems and I'd rather hear from them first.

00:27:06.920 --> 00:27:09.650
Uh, again, that doesn't mean that if at the end of,

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:11.450
uh, a discussion and, uh,

00:27:11.450 --> 00:27:14.765
after multiple attempts to try to draw what I feel would

00:27:14.765 --> 00:27:18.140
be the best answer out from them if they fail to recognize especially a witness,

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:20.600
uh, then we do need to intervene,

00:27:20.600 --> 00:27:23.750
we do need to call attention to that and tell them the right thing to do.

00:27:23.750 --> 00:27:26.900
You don't want them to leave that space thinking that, you know,

00:27:26.900 --> 00:27:30.830
knowing that or you know that they did something that potentially could harm the patient.

00:27:30.830 --> 00:27:31.150
Right.

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:33.470
You don't want them leaving that space thinking that, uh,

00:27:33.470 --> 00:27:36.920
uh, that was the right thing to do because that will lead to, uh, patient harm.

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:37.325
Uh-huh.

00:27:37.325 --> 00:27:38.630
So you can't let that happen.

00:27:38.630 --> 00:27:41.780
But in general, especially with experienced health care professionals,

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:43.640
there's someone who can draw the answer from it.

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:46.640
It's much more powerful that comes from them rather than comes to them.

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:49.040
Yeah. So we're talking a lot about drawing

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:52.010
assessment of performance from the people being debriefed,

00:27:52.010 --> 00:27:53.600
but the other thing that this, uh,

00:27:53.600 --> 00:27:56.960
strategy was related to was avoiding qualitative statements.

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:59.855
Can you give us an example of what that really means?

00:27:59.855 --> 00:28:04.340
Um, sure. This- this is one I use all the time whenever I talk about this.

00:28:04.340 --> 00:28:07.775
Um, [NOISE] if I have two teams, uh,

00:28:07.775 --> 00:28:11.000
with me and team one is coming out from

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:15.440
the scenario for debriefing and I look at them and I say at some point during that debriefing,

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:16.880
yes, I'm conversing with them,

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:19.130
"Oh, you guys did a great job."

00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:22.370
And then team two comes out and they come up for

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:25.595
their debriefing and somewhere in that conversation I say,

00:28:25.595 --> 00:28:27.440
"Oh, you guys did a good job."

00:28:27.440 --> 00:28:32.300
What ends up happening is even though both teams performed at an adequate level,

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:36.320
the team that heard the word great now is thinking that their performance was much,

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:38.720
much better than the team that heard that word good.

00:28:38.720 --> 00:28:42.110
And so those are two perfectly acceptable adjectives.

00:28:42.110 --> 00:28:44.675
They both indicate, you know, good performance, uh,

00:28:44.675 --> 00:28:47.120
adequate performance, yet I set-up a

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:49.925
dynamic here where that I probably didn't wanna set up.

00:28:49.925 --> 00:28:51.485
There's- there's enough, uh,

00:28:51.485 --> 00:28:55.100
difficulty sometimes in debriefing especially with different groups of people,

00:28:55.100 --> 00:28:58.115
uh, that they're reading a lot into it anyway,

00:28:58.115 --> 00:29:01.025
so my preferences to not add to that.

00:29:01.025 --> 00:29:04.250
Uh, not make it more difficult for them to discern,

00:29:04.250 --> 00:29:06.770
uh, I don't want them to just try to discern for me,

00:29:06.770 --> 00:29:07.940
what their performance is like,

00:29:07.940 --> 00:29:11.300
uh, but I'd rather have it come from them, uh,

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:12.800
and, and bubble up for them because,

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:15.035
again, they're gonna remember it much better.

00:29:15.035 --> 00:29:17.600
Um, and, uh, I think be able

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:20.210
to translate whatever it is we talked about into their practice,

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:21.650
uh, if it comes from them,

00:29:21.650 --> 00:29:25.310
rather than sort of in the grade school teacher type,

00:29:25.310 --> 00:29:29.015
teacher-student type of role where I'm the source of all knowledge,

00:29:29.015 --> 00:29:30.200
they don't know much of anything,

00:29:30.200 --> 00:29:31.550
every time that they have a question,

00:29:31.550 --> 00:29:32.930
they asked me, I answer.

00:29:32.930 --> 00:29:35.960
Um, again, with health care professionals, who are adults,

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:37.370
who are intelligent people,

00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:40.430
experienced people, most of the time they know the answers already.

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:43.580
Yeah, yeah. And I can imagine how that also helps to, uh,

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:45.845
[NOISE] and I've seen how that helps to avoid

00:29:45.845 --> 00:29:48.380
creating defensiveness in debriefings too because you're really

00:29:48.380 --> 00:29:50.360
asking for their own assessment of

00:29:50.360 --> 00:29:53.735
their performance and they're not looking to the debriefer for,

00:29:53.735 --> 00:29:56.240
um, some validation or some feedback.

00:29:56.240 --> 00:29:58.640
Um, the next strategy also talks more about

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:00.770
de- defensiveness and really how to deconstruct

00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:02.480
defensiveness and try to minimize

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:06.335
defensiveness and that's limiting the use of second person pronouns.

00:30:06.335 --> 00:30:07.880
So in this clip, um,

00:30:07.880 --> 00:30:11.075
this is a debriefing separate from the- the one that we just watched.

00:30:11.075 --> 00:30:13.685
Uh, this is the resuscitation of a full-term infant,

00:30:13.685 --> 00:30:15.755
who was in cardiac arrest, um,

00:30:15.755 --> 00:30:18.740
and the team in this scenario struggled to provide timely care.

00:30:18.740 --> 00:30:21.020
And so by this point in the debriefing, um,

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:23.090
it's already been established that several steps of

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:26.795
the resuscitation were delayed relative to the standard of care.

00:30:26.795 --> 00:30:31.095
And so because of that, multiple members of the team are unhappy with their performance.

00:30:31.095 --> 00:30:35.065
So let's watch this clip to see how the debriefer- [NOISE] debriefer handles this,

00:30:35.065 --> 00:30:36.610
um, challenging situations.

00:30:36.610 --> 00:30:39.710
[NOISE]

00:30:39.710 --> 00:30:41.900
So in terms of the ergonomics at the bedside,

00:30:41.900 --> 00:30:43.324
the crowding at the bedside,

00:30:43.324 --> 00:30:45.590
is there anything that can be done about that?

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:48.120
Anything that could have been done about that?

00:30:51.190 --> 00:30:55.820
Well, the patient could had been bagged from this side that I'm on now.

00:30:55.820 --> 00:30:56.330
Uh-huh.

00:30:56.330 --> 00:30:56.585
Okay.

00:30:56.585 --> 00:30:59.585
So that would have freed up space.

00:30:59.585 --> 00:31:03.890
Okay. And what could have led to that not happening in this scenario?

00:31:03.890 --> 00:31:11.755
[NOISE] I think not just not being aware that,

00:31:11.755 --> 00:31:18.087
um, [NOISE] there wasn't enough room for her [NOISE] to really get in there and see,

00:31:18.087 --> 00:31:18.300
Uh-huh.

00:31:18.300 --> 00:31:20.920
Okay. What the- what led to that lack of awareness? [NOISE]

00:31:20.920 --> 00:31:27.235
I guess, I mean not saying anything.

00:31:27.235 --> 00:31:30.410
Okay. And what prevent- what could prevent

00:31:30.410 --> 00:31:32.750
an individual in a situation

00:31:32.750 --> 00:31:35.000
like this from speaking up about they don't have enough room?

00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:37.520
[NOISE]

00:31:37.520 --> 00:31:39.620
If so focused on that task.

00:31:39.620 --> 00:31:41.270
Okay. [NOISE]

00:31:41.270 --> 00:31:42.395
I think it's hard to do.

00:31:42.395 --> 00:31:45.680
It's hard for me to speak up sometimes when [OVERLAPPING]

00:31:45.680 --> 00:31:46.700
Why is it hard?

00:31:46.700 --> 00:31:52.280
Because I don't wanna let the team down or I don't want people to think that I can't do

00:31:52.280 --> 00:31:59.060
something when I can do it but the circumstances aren't exactly right,

00:31:59.060 --> 00:32:02.630
and sometimes that's just really hard for me to verbalize.

00:32:02.630 --> 00:32:04.850
[NOISE] But the most important person is the patient

00:32:04.850 --> 00:32:07.625
and they're the ones that really need at the line. So,

00:32:07.625 --> 00:32:08.210
That's true.

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:11.960
I feel like we have to find a way to get over that and took it over,

00:32:11.960 --> 00:32:18.000
you know, and to be okay with switching roles and saying I can't get this done right now.

00:32:18.580 --> 00:32:22.850
Anything else that any other member of this team could have done to determine that

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:28.055
the int- intravascular access was not yet secured?

00:32:28.055 --> 00:32:30.650
I think the team leader could've checked in.

00:32:30.650 --> 00:32:31.580
Okay.

00:32:31.580 --> 00:32:32.270
Earlier.

00:32:32.270 --> 00:32:34.040
Team leader could have acquired about that?

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:34.790
Uh-huh.

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:37.445
Okay. Anybody else on the team could have acquired about that, right?

00:32:37.445 --> 00:32:42.034
I think I definitely could have because when you're doing ventilations and compressions,

00:32:42.034 --> 00:32:44.075
it's hard to do anything else.

00:32:44.075 --> 00:32:44.705
Okay.

00:32:44.705 --> 00:32:46.340
But I wasn't doing either.

00:32:46.340 --> 00:32:52.850
So I could have checked and saw what the status of the fluids and the line placement was.

00:32:52.850 --> 00:32:55.010
There's a number of things that could have been done

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:57.470
differently to perhaps avoid that situation.

00:32:57.470 --> 00:33:01.595
[NOISE] Okay.

00:33:01.595 --> 00:33:05.405
So one of the things I noticed in that clip is that you can even tell from the-

00:33:05.405 --> 00:33:07.910
the body language of some of the people being debriefed

00:33:07.910 --> 00:33:10.160
that they were really unhappy and really defensive.

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:12.260
And in general, debriefing professionals,

00:33:12.260 --> 00:33:14.540
practicing healthcare professionals or people

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:17.120
even outside of health care who might be experts in their field and

00:33:17.120 --> 00:33:19.790
have experienced a difficult situation or

00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:22.730
one in which they made mistakes can be really challenging.

00:33:22.730 --> 00:33:25.010
Um, people can often be

00:33:25.010 --> 00:33:28.400
defensive in those envi- in those situations when they're talking about

00:33:28.400 --> 00:33:31.550
their performance and they're examining their performance and even in this type of

00:33:31.550 --> 00:33:35.240
situation where you might have video of what they've done,

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:37.340
um, to- to show them back.

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:41.225
Um, so how do you approach those types of situations?

00:33:41.225 --> 00:33:43.580
Well, I think the reason you're concerned about

00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:46.970
defensiveness is because defensiveness can impair learning.

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:49.370
And the debriefing is all about learning again.

00:33:49.370 --> 00:33:50.960
Learning strengths, learning [NOISE] weaknesses,

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:54.215
how to replicate the good things and avoid the bad things in the future.

00:33:54.215 --> 00:33:57.170
Uh, but it's also important to remember what we talked about very early on,

00:33:57.170 --> 00:33:59.510
which was that a technical performance debriefing

00:33:59.510 --> 00:34:01.370
is not a critical like incident stress debriefing.

00:34:01.370 --> 00:34:01.700
Uh-huh.

00:34:01.700 --> 00:34:05.630
Um, when I've seen debriefings take place in either high-risk industries,

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:09.470
the debriefings really are not about, um, uh,

00:34:09.470 --> 00:34:10.805
making people feel better,

00:34:10.805 --> 00:34:12.830
they're about making people be [NOISE] better,

00:34:12.830 --> 00:34:15.080
upping the performer at a higher level.

00:34:15.080 --> 00:34:18.590
And so in an instance like that where the team did [NOISE] not perform,

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:21.380
at least in that phase of the patient care,

00:34:21.380 --> 00:34:23.555
the team did not perform an optimal level,

00:34:23.555 --> 00:34:25.775
it doesn't surprise me that they are unhappy.

00:34:25.775 --> 00:34:28.310
In fact, I would expect that they would be unhappy,

00:34:28.310 --> 00:34:32.060
and [NOISE] I would be much more concerned if they were happy with poor performance.

00:34:32.060 --> 00:34:32.480
Uh-huh.

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:33.770
That tells me that we've got

00:34:33.770 --> 00:34:37.550
some actually significant issues that neither the seriousness with

00:34:37.550 --> 00:34:39.380
which they're taking their participation in

00:34:39.380 --> 00:34:42.740
that particular scenario or they just don't understand,

00:34:42.740 --> 00:34:46.790
uh, in general, how important it is to, uh,

00:34:46.790 --> 00:34:51.020
be able to uncover the root causes of the weaknesses that become manifest.

00:34:51.020 --> 00:34:54.725
Um, one of the things we did see in that clip is that, um, uh,

00:34:54.725 --> 00:34:57.530
going back to some of our prior discussion,

00:34:57.530 --> 00:35:00.760
uh, the team [NOISE] did not stay at a very superficial level.

00:35:00.760 --> 00:35:01.370
Uh-huh.

00:35:01.370 --> 00:35:04.595
Okay, yes, the line did not go in in a timely manner.

00:35:04.595 --> 00:35:07.190
Uh, what we were able to do the course that

00:35:07.190 --> 00:35:10.010
debriefing it does actually drill down to the underlying root causes,

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.885
and there were actually [NOISE] a number of those that were,

00:35:11.885 --> 00:35:15.100
uh, uh, elicited from the people being debriefed.

00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:19.645
So, um, that's really what you wanna be able to get out into the debriefing.

00:35:19.645 --> 00:35:22.400
Um, personally, I think that if you

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:26.495
maintain a professional business-like matter-of-fact approach from your debriefing,

00:35:26.495 --> 00:35:29.390
again, you are not really excited and happy with the team performance, well,

00:35:29.390 --> 00:35:33.540
you're not really sort of down and depressed when the team's not performing well.

00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:35.845
And they're not reading your either,

00:35:35.845 --> 00:35:38.740
they're not hear- they're using your words or using

00:35:38.740 --> 00:35:41.020
your nonverbal communication to assess

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:43.840
their performance but rather they're thinking through it critically,

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:47.685
uh, and then you allow- you lead them through a discussion that allows them to

00:35:47.685 --> 00:35:51.785
uncover the underlying root causes and come up with strategies to overcome those,

00:35:51.785 --> 00:35:54.410
I think that's where we get to the point where actually

00:35:54.410 --> 00:35:57.065
we're going to have teams that are again performing,

00:35:57.065 --> 00:35:59.915
uh, enough, or executing in a flawless manner.

00:35:59.915 --> 00:36:01.265
It doesn't mean that they're perfect,

00:36:01.265 --> 00:36:03.530
but it means that when things don't go well,

00:36:03.530 --> 00:36:05.990
they're able to, uh,

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:07.640
uh, enable contingency plans.

00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:07.850
Uh-huh.

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:11.765
Um, and enact those and falsely execute those.

00:36:11.765 --> 00:36:14.225
Uh-huh. Okay. And in this clip,

00:36:14.225 --> 00:36:16.250
getting back to this particular strategy,

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.230
you really avoided, um,

00:36:18.230 --> 00:36:20.540
in a specific, it seemed very intentional, um,

00:36:20.540 --> 00:36:24.724
using the second person pronouns like you, or you guys,

00:36:24.724 --> 00:36:26.795
or even addressing, uh,

00:36:26.795 --> 00:36:29.615
any of the people in that debriefing by name.

00:36:29.615 --> 00:36:34.355
Can you tell us how that strategy helps to minimize defensiveness during a debriefing?

00:36:34.355 --> 00:36:39.200
Sure. I think we need to avoid the use of the- the second person pronoun you,

00:36:39.200 --> 00:36:44.120
uh, the people being debriefed can be much more objective about their performance.

00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:48.610
Um, either way, they're talking about themselves or talking about, uh, their colleagues.

00:36:48.610 --> 00:36:51.100
Um, because you're not putting them on the spot.

00:36:51.100 --> 00:36:52.870
You're talking about, uh,

00:36:52.870 --> 00:36:55.870
the physician in that particular, uh, you've got,

00:36:55.870 --> 00:36:59.125
for example, rather than Doctor Yamada or Nicole.

00:36:59.125 --> 00:37:02.080
And I've seen that repeatedly and that's actually something we brought in

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:04.718
from- we brought in from watching military debriefings.

00:37:04.718 --> 00:37:05.575
Uh-huh.

00:37:05.575 --> 00:37:09.195
Um, it's especially important when performance of sub-optimal.

00:37:09.195 --> 00:37:12.800
Now sometimes groups themselves will use second person pronouns,

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:14.860
pronouns, that's fine if they're comfortable with that.

00:37:14.860 --> 00:37:19.000
But as the debriefer I try to avoid that as much as possible.

00:37:19.000 --> 00:37:20.920
Um, for example, I would never ask them

00:37:20.920 --> 00:37:23.265
individual in a debriefing, "Why didn't you do that?"

00:37:23.265 --> 00:37:25.240
That really puts them on the spot.

00:37:25.240 --> 00:37:28.070
If they're not defensive already they're going to become defensive.

00:37:28.070 --> 00:37:30.120
Instead I would ask the team, you know,

00:37:30.120 --> 00:37:32.680
if it's- if it's about the physician, for example,

00:37:32.680 --> 00:37:36.400
I'd say, what did the physician needing a resuscitation just do.

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:39.865
And once we do that and I'm

00:37:39.865 --> 00:37:43.530
referring to the- the physician in the third person rather than by name,

00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:46.900
actually it makes it much easier for that physician to talk about

00:37:46.900 --> 00:37:51.670
his or her personal actions and whether they were good or bad for the patient.

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:56.255
Um, it also enables the people- other people being debriefed who

00:37:56.255 --> 00:37:58.450
may not usually be in a position where they're often

00:37:58.450 --> 00:38:01.700
critique to a position or to someone who's more senior than they are.

00:38:01.700 --> 00:38:04.985
And they can talk about that physician in the third person and it makes it

00:38:04.985 --> 00:38:08.690
easier for them to offer their own observations about that individual's performance.

00:38:08.690 --> 00:38:13.085
So, um, the- the key is to try to encourage discussion,

00:38:13.085 --> 00:38:15.365
encourage self-reflection, uh, avoid

00:38:15.365 --> 00:38:18.275
defensiveness because that tends to inhibit those things.

00:38:18.275 --> 00:38:20.910
Um, but then follow it up with a series of questions

00:38:20.910 --> 00:38:23.315
where you actually begin to drill down to the root causes.

00:38:23.315 --> 00:38:26.810
So as- as you've said several times now in the course, this webinar.

00:38:26.810 --> 00:38:32.135
The overall goal is to be able to train these teams so they can debrief themselves.

00:38:32.135 --> 00:38:34.300
Uh, whether it be debriefing simulated events.

00:38:34.300 --> 00:38:36.910
Uh, because ultimately that's what's going to

00:38:36.910 --> 00:38:40.880
allow all of us collectively to enhance our performance.

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:45.310
So we've talked a lot about these strategies in the context of taking

00:38:45.310 --> 00:38:48.965
care of patients or even in the context of running simulations.

00:38:48.965 --> 00:38:53.920
Can you, um, comment a little bit about how you see these types of strategies and

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:55.865
the things that we've- that we do talk about in

00:38:55.865 --> 00:38:59.065
our online course being translated even outside of healthcare.

00:38:59.065 --> 00:39:05.735
Sure. I think the ability to objectively, uh, self-reflect,

00:39:05.735 --> 00:39:10.275
and critique one's own performance as well as to objectively and, uh,

00:39:10.275 --> 00:39:13.355
in a non-punitive way talk about college performance,

00:39:13.355 --> 00:39:17.480
is what allows individuals and teams to overall perform at a high level.

00:39:17.480 --> 00:39:20.900
I've certainly seen that in industries outside of health care, uh,

00:39:20.900 --> 00:39:24.040
where either the lives of other individuals

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:27.070
or the lives of the debriefees themselves are at stake.

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:29.365
But I've even seen it in things like, uh,

00:39:29.365 --> 00:39:32.705
or in activities such as athletics.

00:39:32.705 --> 00:39:36.305
Um, where the best athletes tend to be the one to

00:39:36.305 --> 00:39:40.120
instead of falling apart or not recognizing, uh,

00:39:40.120 --> 00:39:45.094
what their only misses are they're able to objectively assess those weaknesses,

00:39:45.094 --> 00:39:47.800
and then develop strategies perhaps in concert with

00:39:47.800 --> 00:39:51.020
a coach but develop strategies to overcome those weaknesses.

00:39:51.020 --> 00:39:53.825
Um, those are the athletes who can handle failure.

00:39:53.825 --> 00:39:55.985
This failure always comes to an athlete at some point,

00:39:55.985 --> 00:40:00.715
and they're the ones who are able to go beyond that in actually excel at what they do.

00:40:00.715 --> 00:40:02.545
Okay. All right.

00:40:02.545 --> 00:40:05.400
I think with that we'll turn it back over, um, to Jackie.

00:40:05.400 --> 00:40:07.570
Okay. So our first question is how can

00:40:07.570 --> 00:40:11.080
these techniques be applied to scenarios outside of emergency care,

00:40:11.080 --> 00:40:15.700
for example, chronic inpatient care or even outside of health care?

00:40:16.110 --> 00:40:21.605
Um, in terms of outside of

00:40:21.605 --> 00:40:23.010
health care I think one of

00:40:23.010 --> 00:40:27.125
the best examples I can give actually involved very junior people.

00:40:27.125 --> 00:40:32.135
Um, I'm the faculty athletic fellow is sort of a liaison between women's, uh,

00:40:32.135 --> 00:40:35.875
indoor volleyball team here at Stanford in the- the faculty,

00:40:35.875 --> 00:40:41.105
and their coaching staff uses these strategies extremely well.

00:40:41.105 --> 00:40:44.870
Um, think of a coach as someone who's always telling somebody what to do.

00:40:44.870 --> 00:40:51.220
Uh, well, what I have seen Coach Hambly do and his staff do exceedingly well is,

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:53.170
rather than always be telling the players what they

00:40:53.170 --> 00:40:55.490
shouldn't be doing or what they shouldn't be doing,

00:40:55.490 --> 00:40:58.855
um, in the huddle it before the game, before the match,

00:40:58.855 --> 00:41:02.205
during the match, after the match, he'll ask them, uh,

00:41:02.205 --> 00:41:05.074
for their assessment of their own performance,

00:41:05.074 --> 00:41:07.520
and what if anything they needed you differently.

00:41:07.520 --> 00:41:11.245
And these are 18-22 year old young women who are very good at doing this.

00:41:11.245 --> 00:41:13.930
So they just won a national championship last year in fact.

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:17.660
Um, so when I see that kind of ability to self-reflect,

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:20.195
analyze and come up with strategies

00:41:20.195 --> 00:41:23.365
to perform at a higher level in- in each group like that,

00:41:23.365 --> 00:41:26.035
I think that certainly that's something that all of us,

00:41:26.035 --> 00:41:28.425
uh, throughout our lives should be able to do.

00:41:28.425 --> 00:41:31.120
Um, other high-risk industries do this quite

00:41:31.120 --> 00:41:33.815
well effectively they do much better than we do in health care,

00:41:33.815 --> 00:41:35.470
quite on soon they've been doing it for a long time,

00:41:35.470 --> 00:41:37.875
NASA has been doing this for 50 years or 60 years.

00:41:37.875 --> 00:41:43.925
So in any sort of environment where individual and team performance is important.

00:41:43.925 --> 00:41:48.340
Uh, the ability of the individuals to self-reflect and the ability of

00:41:48.340 --> 00:41:49.740
the team to reflect to put

00:41:49.740 --> 00:41:53.165
those self-reflections together to come up with an assessment of team performance.

00:41:53.165 --> 00:41:55.570
And again, look at what did we do well?

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:58.180
How can we replicate those things in the future?

00:41:58.180 --> 00:42:00.915
And what did we not do very well?

00:42:00.915 --> 00:42:02.560
What weaknesses became manifest?

00:42:02.560 --> 00:42:04.680
And what strategies can we collectively come

00:42:04.680 --> 00:42:07.060
up with so that we can avoid those things in the future?

00:42:07.060 --> 00:42:10.555
Um, It really applies across the board.

00:42:10.555 --> 00:42:15.185
Okay. Um, our next question is, uh,

00:42:15.185 --> 00:42:17.135
the presenters indicated that working from

00:42:17.135 --> 00:42:20.785
a recording is far preferable to going from memory.

00:42:20.785 --> 00:42:23.490
And If they don't record the actual in the moment

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:26.390
resuscitations to review on at a later date,

00:42:26.390 --> 00:42:28.890
do you have any suggestions?

00:42:28.890 --> 00:42:31.150
Yeah. That's a common challenge that we hear

00:42:31.150 --> 00:42:33.320
about from people who are running debriefings.

00:42:33.320 --> 00:42:34.865
Um, and I think you know,

00:42:34.865 --> 00:42:36.850
as- as we tried to show in these,

00:42:36.850 --> 00:42:40.295
um, debriefings, it is ideal to have that objective record, uh,

00:42:40.295 --> 00:42:43.660
to reference too so that everybody can see both the- end of

00:42:43.660 --> 00:42:45.640
the entire group of people being debriefed as well as

00:42:45.640 --> 00:42:48.010
the debriefer can really see that performance,

00:42:48.010 --> 00:42:52.020
um, and share that understanding before really discussing that performance.

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:55.320
Um, but oftentimes that may not be available for whatever reason

00:42:55.320 --> 00:42:58.660
whether it's time or technology, um, capabilities.

00:42:58.660 --> 00:43:02.560
And so I think just at least having some type of an objective record

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:06.460
of what happened is the- is the goal of what you might want to start with.

00:43:06.460 --> 00:43:08.225
So even if it's not recorded,

00:43:08.225 --> 00:43:10.740
perhaps it's written you can take, uh, you know,

00:43:10.740 --> 00:43:13.235
in a- in an emergency situation oftentimes people are

00:43:13.235 --> 00:43:16.115
recording the- on the code sheet the events that happen,

00:43:16.115 --> 00:43:18.520
or if it's not an emergency situation, if it's,

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:21.040
um, another health care situation or again,

00:43:21.040 --> 00:43:22.470
even outside of healthcare,

00:43:22.470 --> 00:43:25.540
just a record of what the series of events were and use that

00:43:25.540 --> 00:43:28.675
as the shared understanding of the team of really what actually happened.

00:43:28.675 --> 00:43:30.910
And then the goal of the debriefing is to dig down

00:43:30.910 --> 00:43:33.700
into why does events happened as they did?

00:43:33.700 --> 00:43:36.090
And what the team might have been able to do differently.

00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:38.950
Um, or again, if it was a good performance but

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:42.525
the team really did well in that situation and to try to replicate that.

00:43:42.525 --> 00:43:46.120
And I think that's really important for folks to understand that if

00:43:46.120 --> 00:43:49.705
there is no objective record of individual and team performance,

00:43:49.705 --> 00:43:53.230
and if you are trying to reflect on your own performance

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:56.650
as you were involved in that particular event, um,

00:43:56.650 --> 00:44:00.780
one- especially if it's- if you're trying to perform under intense time pressure,

00:44:00.780 --> 00:44:03.755
your ability to remember things while you are-

00:44:03.755 --> 00:44:07.395
you have perhaps a life at stake or some other serious issue,

00:44:07.395 --> 00:44:09.935
and you're doing things actively.

00:44:09.935 --> 00:44:11.350
It's often very difficult,

00:44:11.350 --> 00:44:13.360
your memory may be impaired in terms of

00:44:13.360 --> 00:44:16.270
remembering exactly what you did and when you did it, how you did it.

00:44:16.270 --> 00:44:18.460
Ah, Another thing that can happen is you

00:44:18.460 --> 00:44:21.685
can- if you are part of that you can lose objectivity.

00:44:21.685 --> 00:44:24.730
And so you may be either hypercritical

00:44:24.730 --> 00:44:27.985
or excessively critical of something that you did or your colleagues did.

00:44:27.985 --> 00:44:30.875
Um, or you may not be critical enough and

00:44:30.875 --> 00:44:33.935
you may think that you did a much better job than you actually did.

00:44:33.935 --> 00:44:38.315
So, uh, we encourage people especially if you are- if- if

00:44:38.315 --> 00:44:43.315
we're- if- if- if it's possible to get video it's really important to do that,

00:44:43.315 --> 00:44:46.000
um, and use that because when you're debriefing it really

00:44:46.000 --> 00:44:48.460
resolves a lot of questions about who did what when?

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:51.125
Who said what when? How do they do that?

00:44:51.125 --> 00:44:54.400
Uh, it gives everybody, uh, a very objective,

00:44:54.400 --> 00:44:59.780
very concrete basis upon which to offer critique.

00:45:00.300 --> 00:45:05.665
Thank you. Um, we've had a few questions about,

00:45:05.665 --> 00:45:09.459
um, the timing of debriefing,

00:45:09.459 --> 00:45:12.130
so do you have any guidelines or suggestions for

00:45:12.130 --> 00:45:16.840
a longer-term retrospective debrief so outside of an immediate setting two weeks,

00:45:16.840 --> 00:45:18.400
to a month or more?

00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:21.040
And also is there an extension of time,

00:45:21.040 --> 00:45:24.805
um, that is considered appropriate for a debriefing?

00:45:24.805 --> 00:45:28.810
So if we're- if we're debriefing real life events, um,

00:45:28.810 --> 00:45:33.325
in general you'd like to debrief those as close to the event as possible.

00:45:33.325 --> 00:45:35.710
Um, now you do have to sort some things out,

00:45:35.710 --> 00:45:38.230
I talked about the critical incident stress debriefing.

00:45:38.230 --> 00:45:41.860
Uh, if it's a particularly traumatic event like for us in our field,

00:45:41.860 --> 00:45:43.210
it could be the death of the patient.

00:45:43.210 --> 00:45:45.580
Um, there are some folks who are ready

00:45:45.580 --> 00:45:48.175
to debrief that right away because their whole focus is on,

00:45:48.175 --> 00:45:51.385
what can we do differently to perhaps avoid that are coming in the future,

00:45:51.385 --> 00:45:53.410
and there are other folks who from

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:55.840
an emotional standpoint are not ready to do that at that time.

00:45:55.840 --> 00:45:58.060
And so they may need a critical incident stress debriefing

00:45:58.060 --> 00:46:00.970
before they actually can participate effectively in the debriefing.

00:46:00.970 --> 00:46:04.195
But in general, I think that's- that's smaller number of people.

00:46:04.195 --> 00:46:08.860
Um, so ideally when memories are fresh, get everyone together.

00:46:08.860 --> 00:46:11.305
Uh, debrief on that again.

00:46:11.305 --> 00:46:15.850
Um, sometimes it's not possible to spend much time doing that, and so,

00:46:15.850 --> 00:46:17.305
uh, when it isn't possible,

00:46:17.305 --> 00:46:20.335
try to cover perhaps the most important one or two points.

00:46:20.335 --> 00:46:22.450
And again, not just very superficially,

00:46:22.450 --> 00:46:26.230
but try to drill down a bit on those and the underlying root causes,

00:46:26.230 --> 00:46:31.990
keep- ask people to create a list of other things that occurred in the scheduling events,

00:46:31.990 --> 00:46:35.140
uh, where you actually can bring people together in the future,

00:46:35.140 --> 00:46:38.020
uh, and to go over things in more detail.

00:46:38.020 --> 00:46:41.860
Uh, but again objective records are very helpful,

00:46:41.860 --> 00:46:45.880
um, and the ability to- at least, uh,

00:46:45.880 --> 00:46:48.130
if you're going to delay the briefing to at least

00:46:48.130 --> 00:46:50.575
cover one or two very important aspects of that,

00:46:50.575 --> 00:46:52.960
I think can become critical and maintaining

00:46:52.960 --> 00:46:55.615
people's interest in helping them to, you know.

00:46:55.615 --> 00:46:57.100
Hopefully if- if there are weaknesses,

00:46:57.100 --> 00:47:00.820
avoid those because the same thing could happen in the next hour, the next day.

00:47:00.820 --> 00:47:04.510
Um, and if- if there are particular strengths have become manifest,

00:47:04.510 --> 00:47:06.985
you wanna be able to help them replicate those,

00:47:06.985 --> 00:47:09.190
uh, and you need a teacher also.

00:47:09.190 --> 00:47:11.950
Okay. And then long the same lines,

00:47:11.950 --> 00:47:14.050
ideally how often should you debrief?

00:47:14.050 --> 00:47:15.250
After every event?

00:47:15.250 --> 00:47:16.825
After each new patient?

00:47:16.825 --> 00:47:19.120
How often do you debrief?

00:47:19.120 --> 00:47:24.190
I think in a perfect world we are debriefing, you know, all the time.

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:28.390
Um, but I think because debriefing does take people away from care,

00:47:28.390 --> 00:47:29.440
it can be challenging.

00:47:29.440 --> 00:47:32.620
So the, um, clearly the- the times then

00:47:32.620 --> 00:47:36.280
we debriefed the most often at least in health care is after a mistake is made,

00:47:36.280 --> 00:47:39.100
um, or after a critical event in which a patient,

00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:41.530
um, patient status may have changed acutely.

00:47:41.530 --> 00:47:45.580
Um, but really I would see debriefing and even the strategies that we use in

00:47:45.580 --> 00:47:50.770
debriefings aren't limited to only those discussions that we title as debriefings,

00:47:50.770 --> 00:47:53.080
I think a lot of these strategies can apply

00:47:53.080 --> 00:47:55.810
to just having conversations with one another,

00:47:55.810 --> 00:47:57.655
about trying to uncover, um,

00:47:57.655 --> 00:48:01.555
how we as a team could continue to perform well or- or improve our performance.

00:48:01.555 --> 00:48:03.760
So for example, something like, um,

00:48:03.760 --> 00:48:07.600
avoiding qualitative statements and- and try to minimize defensiveness.

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:09.010
Sometimes when I'm talking to my colleagues,

00:48:09.010 --> 00:48:10.990
even in very short discussions about

00:48:10.990 --> 00:48:13.405
something that I did or something that we did as a team,

00:48:13.405 --> 00:48:17.530
I employ those strategies with the goal of really just trying to learn how to do better.

00:48:17.530 --> 00:48:22.360
Um, but I think as a starting point to that specific questions specifically,

00:48:22.360 --> 00:48:24.505
um, I think, you know, after, uh,

00:48:24.505 --> 00:48:26.440
an acute event when a patient status

00:48:26.440 --> 00:48:30.370
change- changes is really the- the clear starting point,

00:48:30.370 --> 00:48:31.840
but then learning how to disseminate

00:48:31.840 --> 00:48:34.900
these strategies in discussions beyond that is really our goal.

00:48:34.900 --> 00:48:37.330
Remember very important point is especially for

00:48:37.330 --> 00:48:40.015
those of you who are listening in our health care.

00:48:40.015 --> 00:48:43.585
Um, when you debrief for real life health care event, um,

00:48:43.585 --> 00:48:49.690
you want to first have some sort of policy or procedure in place as to how to do that.

00:48:49.690 --> 00:48:51.310
And I would recommend that you talk to

00:48:51.310 --> 00:48:53.380
the risk managers at your hospital, or your clinic,

00:48:53.380 --> 00:48:55.255
or your school, uh,

00:48:55.255 --> 00:48:57.355
to go- to know how to go about that.

00:48:57.355 --> 00:48:59.320
And that's not to say that we want to practice

00:48:59.320 --> 00:49:01.930
defensive medicine and cover up our weaknesses fighting means.

00:49:01.930 --> 00:49:05.110
Uh, if we determine that a weakness has become

00:49:05.110 --> 00:49:06.520
manifested and as resulted in

00:49:06.520 --> 00:49:08.680
patient harm and of course we're going to talk to that patient,

00:49:08.680 --> 00:49:11.500
we're going to talk to that patient's family about the significance of that.

00:49:11.500 --> 00:49:13.240
But what we want to be able to do during

00:49:13.240 --> 00:49:16.060
any briefing is we want people to be able to speak freely.

00:49:16.060 --> 00:49:18.460
And if they feel that they are perhaps,

00:49:18.460 --> 00:49:21.220
uh, placing themselves at, uh,

00:49:21.220 --> 00:49:23.515
legal risk or their institution at legal risk,

00:49:23.515 --> 00:49:28.465
they may not speak freely and we may never uncover the root cause of a particular issue.

00:49:28.465 --> 00:49:31.720
And so it's really to protect the confidentiality of that group,

00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:32.995
get as much information,

00:49:32.995 --> 00:49:35.830
accurate information as possible out into that discussion,

00:49:35.830 --> 00:49:38.650
come up with what the root causes are for that event,

00:49:38.650 --> 00:49:40.585
and then develop solutions around those.

00:49:40.585 --> 00:49:44.590
And then of course once that's determined if- if we need to inform the patient,

00:49:44.590 --> 00:49:45.970
the patient's family that perhaps,

00:49:45.970 --> 00:49:47.020
yes we were in error,

00:49:47.020 --> 00:49:49.270
uh, then we need to go ahead and do that.

00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:52.690
So again, talk to your legal team, uh,

00:49:52.690 --> 00:49:54.520
It's not about practicing defensive medicine,

00:49:54.520 --> 00:49:56.230
it's actually by getting the best information

00:49:56.230 --> 00:49:58.180
out and then doing the right thing for the patient.

00:49:58.180 --> 00:50:02.620
Okay. Our next question here is in a debrief setting,

00:50:02.620 --> 00:50:04.450
how would you approach a person who routinely

00:50:04.450 --> 00:50:07.240
cannot admit to or see that they'd made a mistake?

00:50:07.240 --> 00:50:09.400
And how would you deal with colleagues who refuse

00:50:09.400 --> 00:50:12.340
formal or critical stress debriefing in a less formal,

00:50:12.340 --> 00:50:15.200
but more helpful way for them?

00:50:16.980 --> 00:50:19.570
I think those are two separate questions.

00:50:19.570 --> 00:50:22.570
[LAUGHTER]. Um, so the first one,

00:50:22.570 --> 00:50:25.750
ah, is regarded- is regarding just common challenges.

00:50:25.750 --> 00:50:30.790
Um, people who can't see their own weaknesses or perhaps refuse to see

00:50:30.790 --> 00:50:33.670
their own weaknesses or acknowledged them or clearly don't wanna talk

00:50:33.670 --> 00:50:37.000
about them and kind of just lumping them all in that defensive category.

00:50:37.000 --> 00:50:40.090
Um, I think that situation is really

00:50:40.090 --> 00:50:43.735
a place where having an objective record can be very very valuable.

00:50:43.735 --> 00:50:45.535
And that's why especially,

00:50:45.535 --> 00:50:47.140
um, in training, uh,

00:50:47.140 --> 00:50:51.730
circumstances when- when the environment allows or, you know,

00:50:51.730 --> 00:50:54.250
if there's an ability to setup video,

00:50:54.250 --> 00:50:57.550
that video is your best friend as the debriefer in that situation

00:50:57.550 --> 00:51:00.790
because now you have a record where you don't have to get

00:51:00.790 --> 00:51:04.150
in a situation where the debriefers were counting what happened if perhaps

00:51:04.150 --> 00:51:08.905
that defensive person is arguing with what actually happened or what they said or did.

00:51:08.905 --> 00:51:10.840
Um, you can really just refer to the video,

00:51:10.840 --> 00:51:12.580
and let everybody that you briefing see that,

00:51:12.580 --> 00:51:15.115
and then just talk about it in a very objective manner.

00:51:15.115 --> 00:51:16.585
You now what happened, um,

00:51:16.585 --> 00:51:17.650
the series of events,

00:51:17.650 --> 00:51:21.445
what were the circumstances that led to those different series of events.

00:51:21.445 --> 00:51:23.260
But then, also, um,

00:51:23.260 --> 00:51:25.480
being careful i think to avoid perhaps directing

00:51:25.480 --> 00:51:27.700
all of the questions specifically to that person,

00:51:27.700 --> 00:51:30.430
even though you as a debriefer may think that that person

00:51:30.430 --> 00:51:33.550
had the primary responsibility for that series of events.

00:51:33.550 --> 00:51:36.070
Um, but asking the group as a whole to

00:51:36.070 --> 00:51:39.235
analyze the team's performance in that situation, um,

00:51:39.235 --> 00:51:42.490
and then using strategies like we cover here where you're avoiding the second person,

00:51:42.490 --> 00:51:46.284
you're really inviting everybody to discuss, um, their perceptions,

00:51:46.284 --> 00:51:50.650
um, and their understanding of how the team performed in that particular situation.

00:51:50.650 --> 00:51:55.000
That's a common concern that people won't want to talk about their errors,

00:51:55.000 --> 00:51:56.665
will be really defensive.

00:51:56.665 --> 00:51:58.720
Uh, again, we've used video for a long time.

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:00.925
I've been debriefing for over two decades now,

00:52:00.925 --> 00:52:05.245
and my experience with that is actually that's a fairly rare occurrence.

00:52:05.245 --> 00:52:06.865
Um, because the video,

00:52:06.865 --> 00:52:08.755
you really can't argue with it, it's there.

00:52:08.755 --> 00:52:10.960
And the vast majority of the time,

00:52:10.960 --> 00:52:13.060
even when people perform poorly, uh,

00:52:13.060 --> 00:52:15.820
they're more than willing to talk about it because it's there,

00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:19.675
everybody sees it, and they want to find a way to avoid that in the future.

00:52:19.675 --> 00:52:23.020
So I haven't seen that to be a common problem at all,

00:52:23.020 --> 00:52:27.475
even though it gets talked about quite a bit within the health care debriefing community.

00:52:27.475 --> 00:52:30.280
I'm sorry, there was a second part to the question also.

00:52:30.280 --> 00:52:31.735
[LAUGHTER]. Um, yeah.

00:52:31.735 --> 00:52:35.335
So how would you debrief colleagues who refuse

00:52:35.335 --> 00:52:39.805
formal critical stress debriefing in a less formal but still helpful way?

00:52:39.805 --> 00:52:42.910
Yeah. I think that those kinds of things- some- sometimes on

00:52:42.910 --> 00:52:46.660
a rare occasion that may actually occur during a debriefing itself,

00:52:46.660 --> 00:52:48.910
with it just becomes manifests as somebody's having

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:51.655
a really difficult time and really can't participate.

00:52:51.655 --> 00:52:56.095
And so- our strategies are to actually,

00:52:56.095 --> 00:52:59.530
um, send the rest of your thought for a quick break,

00:52:59.530 --> 00:53:01.855
and then talk to that person one-on-one,

00:53:01.855 --> 00:53:04.405
asking if there's somebody- that they can call,

00:53:04.405 --> 00:53:05.920
you can call to help them out,

00:53:05.920 --> 00:53:08.110
um, ask them if they can continue.

00:53:08.110 --> 00:53:10.300
Uh, you can't force anyone, uh,

00:53:10.300 --> 00:53:11.680
to go see someone, uh,

00:53:11.680 --> 00:53:13.060
even though you think they need help,

00:53:13.060 --> 00:53:15.430
but what you can do is you can be compassionate in your approach,

00:53:15.430 --> 00:53:19.495
recognize when they might need help and offer that to them.

00:53:19.495 --> 00:53:21.220
